<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Chosokabe clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1560 CE – 1600 CE (approx.) · capital Okoh, later Urado</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Chosokabe of Tosa, whose lord Motochika conquered nearly all of Shikoku before Toyotomi Hideyoshi cut him back to his home province.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chosokabe were one of seven local warrior families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a>, based at Okoh castle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-kunichika">Chosokabe Kunichika</a> rebuilt their power, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Motochika</a>, who succeeded in 1560, defeated rival families and the Ichijo governors to unify Tosa by 1575.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Motochika</a> relied on part-time farmer-soldiers, the ichiryo gusoku, who kept their armour in the fields ready to fight. He overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima">Awa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanuki-province">Sanuki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo</a> and by 1585 controlled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. He carried out a full land survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a> and in 1597 issued a detailed house code, moving his seat to Urado on the coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hideyoshi's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> in 1585 reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Motochika</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a>, and his heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-nobuchika">Nobuchika</a> died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> campaign in 1586. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Morichika</a> joined the western army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 and lost Tosa, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tosa-domain">Tosa Domain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chosokabe-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Chosokabe clan domain" width="320" height="319"><figcaption>Image: 百楽兎 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanatsukatabami.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1560 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Okoh, later Urado</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chosokabe Kunichika, Chosokabe Motochika, Chosokabe Morichika</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chosokabe clan, Chosokabe of Tosa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1580&amp;focus=chosokabe-clan-domain" aria-label="Chosokabe clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8168 866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chosokabe clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8440 1178h0M8410 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8488 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8488 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=1580&amp;focus=chosokabe-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
